.docname {Chat Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Conversation | One chat, owned by one account, with its messages inside it | "Why is room 2 hot?", 6 messages |
    | Exchange | One question and its answer, stored together | user message + assistant message |
    | History | The latest messages sent to `agent` as context | the last 10 |
    | SSE | Server-Sent Events: one HTTP answer that keeps sending small text frames | `data: {"type":"token","text":"The "}` |
    | Frame | One SSE event, ended by a blank line | a `token` frame |
    | Token | A small piece of the answer, sent as soon as it exists | `"The "` |
    | Citation | A document part the answer is based on | `{"source":"floor-plans.qd", …}` |
    | ObjectId | MongoDB's own id type; never leaves the storage adapter | shown as `"6650c1…"` |
    | Owner | The account in the `sub` of the claims | `3f2b` |

Rust service at `backend/chat`. Axum, MongoDB. It stores conversations, sends each question to `agent` with the recent history, relays the answer to the browser as it is written, and saves the exchange once the answer is whole.

`agent` stores nothing: every `/ask` stands alone. `chat` is the part that remembers.

Role in the system: [Chat](../architecture/chat-architecture.qd). The other side of the stream: [Agent Service](agent.qd).

---

## Layout

Ports and adapters. `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` fails the build when a rule breaks.

| Path | Holds | May not import |
|---|---|---|
| `src/domain/conversation.rs` | `Conversation`, `ChatMessage`, `Citation`, limits, `validate_text`, history window, auto title | axum, mongodb, reqwest, bson, `crate::service`, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/domain/identity.rs` | `GatewayClaims {user_id, raw}` | same |
| `src/service/conversations.rs` | Use cases; the streaming `Exchange` | any framework, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/service/ports.rs` | `ConversationStore`, `AgentClient`, `AgentEvent` | |
| `src/adapters/driving/http_api/` | Claims extractor, handlers, SSE frames, error mapping | `crate::adapters::driven` |
| `src/adapters/driven/persistence/` | MongoDB store, the only file that knows `ObjectId` | |
| `src/adapters/driven/agent.rs` | `AgentService`: calls `agent` `/ask`, reads its SSE stream | |
| `src/adapters/ratelimit.rs` | 300 requests per minute per client IP | |

Other fitness rules:

- `ObjectId` appears only in `adapters/driven/persistence/conversations.rs`. bson writes it as `{"$oid": "…"}`, which is not what the API returns.
- No module uses a blanket `rename_all = "camelCase"`: citation fields are snake_case and must stay so.
- The claims header name comes only from `claims_schema::CLAIMS_HEADER`.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        H["http_api<br/>controllers"] --> S["Conversations"]
        S --> D["domain<br/>rules"]
        S -. "ConversationStore" .-> M["MongoConversations"]
        S -. "AgentClient" .-> A["AgentService"]
        M --> DB[("chatdb.conversations")]
        A -- "POST /ask, x-gateway-claims" --> Ag["agent"]

---

## Routes

All behind the edge at `/chat/*`. The edge must not buffer: Caddy needs `flush_interval -1`, or every token arrives at once at the end.

| Method | Path | Who may call | What it does | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/health`, `/metrics` | Anyone | Probe, Prometheus | `200` |
| `POST` | `/conversations` | Signed in | New empty conversation; optional `title` | `201` conversation |
| `GET` | `/conversations` | Signed in | Own conversations, newest `updatedAt` first, no messages | `{conversations: [...]}` |
| `GET` | `/conversations/{id}` | Owner | One conversation with every message | conversation |
| `PATCH` | `/conversations/{id}` | Owner | Rename; `title` required | conversation |
| `DELETE` | `/conversations/{id}` | Owner | Delete | `204` |
| `POST` | `/conversations/{id}/messages` | Owner | Ask; the answer streams back | SSE |

### Who is the owner

`GatewayClaims` in `http_api/claims.rs`:

1. No `x-gateway-claims` header, or empty: `401 Missing authentication token`.
2. Not base64 JSON (any of the four base64 alphabets is accepted): `401 Invalid authentication token`.
3. No `sub`, or blank: `401 Authentication token is missing an account id`.
4. Otherwise `user_id = sub`, and the raw header is kept to forward to `agent`.

Every query on one conversation filters on `{_id, userId}` together. Someone else's conversation is a miss, so the answer is `404 Conversation not found`, never `403`: a `403` would tell a stranger the id exists. An id that is not a valid ObjectId is also a miss, `404`, not `400`.

### Errors

`DomainError::describe` names every error once, so the same error reads the same as a status or as an SSE frame:

| Variant | Status | `type` | Example message |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Validation` | 400 | `Validation Error` | `content must be a non-empty string` |
| `Unauthorized` | 401 | `Unauthorized Error` | `Missing authentication token` |
| `NotFound` | 404 | `Not Found Error` | `Conversation not found` |
| `Conflict` | 409 | `Conflict Error` | `Conversation message limit reached` |
| `BadGateway` | 502 | `Bad Gateway Error` | `Could not reach agent` |
| `Internal` | 500 | `Internal Server Error` | Generic text; the cause is only logged |

A missing or unreadable JSON body is read as `{}`: the field check then gives its own message, not a framework error.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `MONGO_URI` | `mongodb://localhost:27017/chatdb` | Database; name from the URI path, else `chatdb` |
| `AGENT_URL` | `http://agent:3000` | Base of `agent`'s `/ask` |
| `HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES` | `10` | Messages sent as history. Read once at start; `0` or not a number stops the service at start |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port |

Fixed in code:

| Limit | Value | Applies to |
|---|---|---|
| `MAX_MESSAGES` | 100 | Messages in one conversation |
| `MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH` | 8000 characters | One question, after trimming |
| `MAX_TITLE_LENGTH` | 120 characters | A title, after trimming |
| `DEFAULT_TITLE` | `New chat` | A conversation created without `title` |
| Agent connect timeout | 5 s | Opening the connection to `agent` |
| Agent read timeout | 60 s | Each wait for the next bytes of the stream |

There is no total timeout on the `agent` call: a long answer may take minutes. The read timeout still ends a stream that stops sending.

---

## What it stores

One collection, `conversations`. Messages live **inside** their conversation, so one write updates both.

```js
{"_id": ObjectId("6650c1aa…"), "userId": "3f2b", "title": "Why is room 2 hot?",
 "messages": [
   {"_id": ObjectId("6650c1b0…"), "role": "user", "content": "Why is room 2 hot?", "createdAt": ISODate(…)},
   {"_id": ObjectId("6650c1b1…"), "role": "assistant", "content": "Room 2 reads 31 °C…",
    "citations": [{"chunk_id": "c9", "document_id": "d2", "source": "floor-plans.qd", "section_path": "Rooms"}],
    "createdAt": ISODate(…)}
 ],
 "createdAt": ISODate(…), "updatedAt": ISODate(…)}
```

| Index | Serves |
|---|---|
| `userId` | Owner lookups |
| `userId, updatedAt -1` | The list, newest first |

On the way out the adapter turns every `ObjectId` into a bare hex string and every date into `2026-11-02T10:15:30.123Z`. The API shape is pinned in `schemas/fixtures/chat-conversation.json`; the frontend reads it only through `src/utils/commons/chat.ts`.

`Citation` keeps `agent`'s Python names (`chunk_id`, `document_id`, `source`, `section_path`). A missing `section_path` is written as `null`. A message with no citations has no `citations` key at all.

---

## 1. Asking a question

### End to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as send_message
        participant C as Conversations::send_message
        participant DB as MongoDB
        participant A as AgentService::ask
        participant G as agent
        B->>H: POST /conversations/6650c1aa/messages {content}
        H->>C: send_message(user_id, id, content, claims.raw)
        C->>C: validate_text(content, 8000)
        C->>DB: find_owned: {_id, userId}
        DB-->>C: conversation with 4 messages
        C->>C: has_room_for_exchange: 4 + 2 at most 100
        C->>C: recent_history(10)
        C->>A: ask(question, history, header)
        A->>G: POST /ask {question, history, stream: true}
        G-->>A: 200, headers in
        A-->>C: AnswerStream
        C-->>H: stream of ChatEvent
        H-->>B: 200, text/event-stream
        loop each token
            G-->>A: data: {"type":"token","text":"Room "}
            A->>A: take_frames, parse_frame gives Token
            A-->>C: Token
            C->>C: add text to the answer kept in memory
            C-->>B: data: {"type":"token","text":"Room "}
        end
        G-->>A: data: {"type":"done","answer":…,"citations":[…]}
        A-->>C: Done
        C->>C: answer from done wins over the tokens
        C->>DB: append_exchange: push 2 messages, set updatedAt and title
        DB-->>C: stored assistant message
        C-->>B: data: {"type":"done","message":{…}}

Steps 3 to 12 can still fail with a normal status code: the answer has not started. From step 13 on, the status `200` is sent, so a failure can only arrive as an `error` frame.

### Data at each step

Example: Ada (`sub` `3f2b`) asks "Why is room 2 hot?" in a conversation called `New chat` with no messages.

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `{"content":"  Why is room 2 hot?  "}` |
| 3 | `Why is room 2 hot?`: trimmed, 18 characters, under 8000 |
| 5 | Conversation `6650c1aa…`, title `New chat`, 0 messages |
| 6 | 0 + 2 ≤ 100: room left |
| 7 | `[]`: no history yet |
| 9 | `{"question":"Why is room 2 hot?","history":[],"stream":true}` and header `x-gateway-claims` = Ada's raw header |
| 14 to 18 | Tokens `Room `, `2 reads `, `31 °C [1]`. Kept in memory: `Room 2 reads 31 °C [1]` |
| 19 | `{"type":"done","answer":"Room 2 reads 31 °C.","citations":[{"chunk_id":"c9",…}]}` |
| 21 | Stored text `Room 2 reads 31 °C.`: the agent removed a citation mark it could not back |
| 22 | `$push` user and assistant messages, `$set` `updatedAt` = now and `title` = `Why is room 2 hot?` |
| 24 | `{"type":"done","message":{"_id":"6650c1b1…","role":"assistant","content":"Room 2 reads 31 °C.","citations":[…],"createdAt":"…"}}` |

### Rules of an exchange

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| The text is checked before the conversation is loaded | A bad request costs no database read |
| The cap counts the 2 new messages | A conversation of 99 messages would end at 101 |
| History = the last `HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES` **messages**, not exchanges | With 10, the agent sees the last 5 questions and 5 answers |
| The caller's own header goes to `agent` | `agent` checks rights as the user; `chat` never asks for more than the user may see |
| Nothing is stored before the `done` frame | A broken answer leaves the conversation as it was |
| `answer` on `done` wins over the joined tokens | `agent` can rewrite what the model wrote: it drops citations it cannot back, or text streamed before a tool call. An `agent` that sends no `answer` gets the joined tokens stored |
| Both messages and the title in one `update_one` | Two sends at once cannot lose each other's messages |
| The first exchange of a `New chat` sets the title | The first 120 characters of the question; any later exchange leaves it |

**Example, the cap.** A conversation has 98 messages: 98 + 2 = 100, allowed. Afterwards it has 100: 100 + 2 = 102, so the next ask is `409 Conversation message limit reached`, and `agent` is never called.

**Example, history.** A conversation has 14 messages, `HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES` is 10. `recent_history` skips the first 4 and sends messages 5 to 14, each as `{role, content}`. Citations are not sent.

### Every way it can end

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        S["POST messages"] --> V{"content non-empty, at most 8000?"}
        V -- no --> E400["400"]
        V -- yes --> O{"owned conversation found?"}
        O -- no --> E404["404"]
        O -- yes --> Cap{"room for 2 more?"}
        Cap -- no --> E409["409"]
        Cap -- yes --> Ask{"agent reached and answers 2xx?"}
        Ask -- no --> E502["502 Could not reach agent, or agent returned N"]
        Ask -- yes --> Open["200, stream open"]
        Open --> Next{"next event"}
        Next -- token --> Tok["token frame"] --> Next
        Next -- "unreadable frame or broken connection" --> F1["error frame: agent stream ended unexpectedly"]
        Next -- "stream ends with no done" --> F2["error frame: agent returned an invalid response"]
        Next -- done --> P{"append_exchange"}
        P -- "conversation gone" --> F3["error frame: Conversation not found"]
        P -- "Mongo error" --> F4["error frame: Internal Server Error"]
        P -- stored --> Done["done frame with the stored message"]

None of the error frames stores anything. While the stream is quiet, axum sends an SSE keep-alive comment so proxies do not close it.

---

## 2. Reading the agent's stream

`agent.rs` turns raw bytes into events. Bytes arrive in pieces that do not match frames.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant N as Network
        participant R as SseReader::step
        participant T as take_frames
        participant P as parse_frame
        participant C as Exchange::step
        C->>R: next event?
        R->>R: anything in pending? no
        R->>N: next chunk
        N-->>R: bytes
        R->>R: add to buffer
        R->>T: cut at every blank line
        T-->>R: whole frames, rest stays in buffer
        loop each whole frame
            R->>P: frame bytes
            P->>P: join the data: lines, read JSON
            P-->>R: Token, Done, nothing, or an error
        end
        R-->>C: first pending event

| Frame | Result |
|---|---|
| `{"type":"token","text":"…"}` | `Token`; missing `text` = empty |
| `{"type":"done","answer":"…","citations":[…]}` | `Done`; both fields optional |
| Any other `type` | Skipped, so `agent` can add a frame kind without breaking `chat` |
| A comment like `: ping`, or an empty frame | Skipped |
| Not JSON | An error: the exchange ends with `agent stream ended unexpectedly` |

**Example.** Read 1 brings `data: {"type":"token","te`, read 2 brings `xt":"Room "}\n\n`. After read 1 there is no blank line, so nothing is cut and the bytes wait. After read 2 the whole frame is cut and gives `Token("Room ")`. The buffer stays bytes until the cut, so a character like `°`, 2 bytes, split across reads is not broken.

The frames are pinned in `schemas/fixtures/agent-stream.json`. Its `tolerated` cases must be survived, so the skipping above is a decision, not luck.

---

## 3. The other routes

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as handlers
        participant C as Conversations
        participant DB as MongoDB
        B->>H: POST /conversations {title?}
        H->>C: create(user_id, Absent or Present)
        C->>C: Absent gives New chat, Present is checked
        C->>DB: insert_one
        H-->>B: 201 conversation
        B->>H: GET /conversations
        H->>C: list(user_id)
        C->>DB: find userId, without messages, sort updatedAt -1
        H-->>B: {conversations: [...]}
        B->>H: PATCH /conversations/{id} {title}
        H->>C: rename
        C->>C: validate_text(title, 120)
        C->>DB: find_one_and_update {_id, userId}, set title and updatedAt
        H-->>B: conversation, or 404
        B->>H: DELETE /conversations/{id}
        H->>C: delete
        C->>DB: delete_one {_id, userId}
        H-->>B: 204, or 404

`title` on create has three cases. The handler keeps the body as raw JSON because a typed `Option` cannot tell them apart:

| Body | Result |
|---|---|
| `{}` | Title `New chat` |
| `{"title":"Floor 3"}` | Title `Floor 3`, trimmed |
| `{"title":null}` or `{"title":"  "}` | `400 title must be a non-empty string` |

---

## Tests

```bash
just test chat               # unit, no infra
just test chat-integration   # tests/*.rs against a throwaway Mongo and a mock agent
```

| Where | Checks |
|---|---|
| `domain/conversation.rs` | Bare string ids; snake_case citations; `section_path` null; trim then measure; cap at 98 and 99; history window; title only on the first exchange, cut at 120; the `chat-conversation.json` fixture round-trips, its rejected cases fail |
| `service/conversations.rs` | Tokens then stored message; `answer` wins, else tokens; aborted or unterminated stream stores nothing; unreachable agent fails before the stream; history and header forwarded; cap is `409` before the agent; empty content before any load; someone else's conversation `404` |
| `adapters/driven/agent.rs` | Every `agent-stream.json` stream decodes as meant; tolerated frames survived; a frame split over two reads; a character split over two reads; several frames in one read; keep-alive skipped; bad frame is an error; early end gives no `done` |
| `http_api/claims.rs`, `exceptions.rs` | The four `401` cases; URL-safe base64; each error's status and name; internal causes never shown |
| `tests/api.rs` | The same flows over HTTP with a real Mongo: ids, titles, ordering by `updatedAt`, `404` for strangers and bad ids, streamed answer stored, cleaned answer stored, nothing stored on a broken stream |
| `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` | Layer imports; `ObjectId` confined; no blanket camelCase; claims header name |

---

## Gaps

- **Sends to one conversation are not serialised.** Two questions sent at the same moment each read the same history and both get stored. Only the frontend, which waits for one answer before the next, prevents it.
- **The cap is checked before the answer, not at the write.** Two sends at 98 messages can both pass and leave 102.
